---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import QrLessonOverlay from '../../components/QrLessonOverlay.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="You scanned a QR code | NoRec.US"
  description="NoRec.US documents surveillance systems and the records behind them. A short reminder to treat unknown QR codes like unknown links."
>
  <QrLessonOverlay />

  <main class="qr_shell">
    <section class="qr_intro">
      <p class="eyebrow">YOU SCANNED A QR CODE</p>
      <h1>I wouldn’t make that a habit.</h1>
      <p class="qr_intro_lede">
        <strong>NoRec.US documents surveillance</strong> systems, policies, contracts,
        public records, and the unanswered questions around them.
      </p>
      <p>
        A QR code is just encoded data. The catch is that you usually cannot tell by looking
        at the pattern where it will send you or what the destination will ask your phone,
        browser, or app to do next.
      </p>
      <div class="hero_actions">
        <a class="button button_dark button_big" href="/">Visit NoRec.US →</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="qr_warning">
      <p class="eyebrow">WHY BE CAREFUL?</p>
      <h2>Treat an unknown QR code like an unknown link.</h2>

      <div class="qr_warning_grid">
        <div>
          <strong>Tracking and redirects</strong>
          <p>
            A unique link can record that a particular code was visited and receive ordinary
            connection metadata such as time, IP address, and browser or device information.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <strong>Phishing and payment scams</strong>
          <p>
            A code can send you to a convincing login, parking, payment, delivery, or account
            page that is not operated by the organization you expected.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <strong>Downloads and app actions</strong>
          <p>
            A destination can prompt a file download, open a deep link, launch an app workflow,
            or ask for permissions you did not intend to grant.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <strong>Vulnerable software</strong>
          <p>
            Crafted destinations can target flaws in browsers, apps, or operating systems.
            Simply decoding a normal QR code is not the same thing as being compromised, but
            the destination still deserves the same caution as any other untrusted link.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="qr_media" aria-label="QR-code safety reminder">
      <figure>
        <img
          src="/media/qr/random-qr-superbowl-meme.png"
          alt="Meme about scanning an unknown QR code and discovering an unexpected destination."
          loading="lazy"
        />
        <figcaption>Unknown code, unknown destination.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="qr_intro_rick">
        <img
          src="/media/qr/rickroll-roll.gif"
          alt="Looping reaction image emphasizing that QR destinations can be unexpected."
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="qr_rule">
      <p class="eyebrow">NOREC.US</p>
      <h2>Surveillance, documented.</h2>
      <p>
        NoRec.US follows the records: statutes, contracts, policies, procurement documents,
        audits, public reports, and public-records responses. When the evidence stops, the
        claim stops with it.
      </p>
      <div class="hero_actions">
        <a class="button button_dark button_big" href="/">Visit the homepage →</a>
        <a class="button button_light" href="/sources/">Browse the sources</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
